/gradlew text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.jar binary

# Source files must stay LF in the working tree on every platform so the Linux
# Docker build (and Spotless inside it) does not see CRLF drift from Windows
# checkouts. Without these rules, a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true
# converts the working-tree copy to CRLF and the resulting `docker compose
# build` either fails (Spotless) or burns a re-format diff round-trip.
#
# `*.ts` is here for the same reason and was missed when the TypeScript
# declaration files arrived with the checkJs type-check gate (ADR-0125): the
# blobs are LF, but a Windows checkout without this rule wrote CRLF, and
# Prettier's default `endOfLine: "lf"` then failed `:frontend:prettierCheck` on
# a pristine clone with no local edits. That gate is strict and wired into
# `check`, so it went red for every Windows developer while Linux CI stayed
# green — a lint gate that is red by default is a lint gate people learn to
# ignore. It covers `*.d.ts` too.
*.java text eol=lf
*.kt text eol=lf
*.kts text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
# `*.mjs` is the same language one extension over — the ESM build scripts and flat lint
# configs under `frontend/` — and was missed for the same reason `*.ts` was.
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.xml text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
.editorconfig text eol=lf

# `.env.example` is copied verbatim onto a Linux host as the starting point for
# `.env` (deployment runbook §5.1). deploy.sh then reads IRI_KEYSTORE_HOST_PATH
# back out of that file with grep/cut, so a CRLF checkout hands it a path with a
# trailing carriage return — and the pre-flight aborts with
# "required file missing: /var/iri/secrets/keystore.p12" for a file that is
# plainly sitting right there. Cost an hour of looking at permissions on the
# wrong file. Same class of drift the block above exists to prevent.
.env.example text eol=lf

# Keycloak theme templates ship in the basetool-config bundle and are read by
# FreeMarker inside the container; LF everywhere keeps the bundle byte-stable
# regardless of which platform built it.
*.ftl text eol=lf

# The edge proxy's configuration is bind-mounted into a Linux container and read
# by nginx (ADR-0162). A Windows checkout that produced CRLF would be mounted as
# CRLF into a local test stack, so the line ending is pinned rather than left to
# core.autocrlf — the same reasoning as the Keycloak templates above.
docker/edge/** text eol=lf

# The maintenance page's nginx snippets, for the identical reason, and missed when the block above
# was written: `docker-compose.yml` bind-mounts `docker/maintenance/nginx/http.conf` and
# `server_proxy.conf` into nginx-proxy-manager's `/data/nginx/custom` hook. A local test stack
# started from a Windows checkout mounts the CRLF copy — precisely the case the edge rule exists
# to prevent.
docker/maintenance/** text eol=lf

# Alloy's configuration is bind-mounted into `grafana/alloy` and format-checked with
# `alloy fmt --test`, which `monitoring/README.md` and the monitoring rollout runbook document as
# copy-pasteable commands run against the WORKING COPY (`-v "$PWD/monitoring/alloy:/cfg"`).
# Without this rule a Windows checkout failed them with "file /cfg/config.alloy is not formatted
# correctly" for the line endings alone, on a file with no formatting problem at all — and Linux
# CI stayed green, so it read as a local mistake rather than a missing attribute. Verified
# 2026-09-20 against the pinned v1.19.2: the same file piped through `tr -d '\r'` passes.
*.alloy text eol=lf

# Templates rendered on the host into a file whose own extension is already pinned above:
# `monitoring/alertmanager/alertmanager.yml.tmpl` becomes `alertmanager.yml` via `envsubst`
# (MONITORING_ROLLOUT_RUNBOOK §3.5). `*.yml` carries the rule; `*.yml.tmpl` matches `*.tmpl` and
# nothing covered it.
*.tmpl text eol=lf

# The systemd units and their log rotation. `docs/backup.md` and `docs/deployment.md` copy these
# onto the production host verbatim (`sudo cp /var/iri/code/scripts/iri-backup.service …`), and a
# CRLF copy is not cosmetic there: logrotate rejects the whole file with "lines must begin with a
# keyword or a filename" and skips it in silence, and a carriage return inside `ExecStart=` makes
# systemd report 203/EXEC for a path that is plainly right. Both were observed on the
# rootless-podman host, which received four timers and four logrotate configs with CRLF.
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer text eol=lf
*.logrotate text eol=lf

# `*.py` for the same reason as `*.sh`: the scripts under `scripts/` and `.github/scripts/` carry
# a `#!/usr/bin/env python3` shebang and three of them are marked executable, so a CRLF working
# copy makes the kernel look for an interpreter whose name ends in a carriage return, and report
# 203/EXEC for one that is plainly installed. The blobs are LF and Linux CI checks out LF, so this
# can only ever bite a Windows working tree — which is the case this whole file exists for.
*.py text eol=lf

# `gradle/libs.versions.toml` is rewritten in place by `./gradlew refreshVersions`, which appends
# `## ⬆ = "…"` markers line by line. On a CRLF working copy that turns an annotate-only run
# into a whole-file line-ending diff. `.gitleaks.toml` is read inside a container, by the same
# reasoning as the edge configuration above.
*.toml text eol=lf
